  "name": "Lundu (Afro-Brazilian dance-song)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Afro-Brazilian couple dance of colonial Brazil, described from the eighteenth century with hand-clapping and the umbigada, that became in the nineteenth century a comic, syncopated song genre of salons and theatres, the lundu-canção, sung alongside the modinha. Xisto Bahia's Isto é bom, sung by Baiano for Casa Edison in 1902, is usually cited as the first Brazilian commercial record. The lundu dance survives on the island of Marajó in Pará.",
  "recipe": "classic early-1900s lundu, functional Song, Brazilian song branch. solo with instrumental accompaniment. low mid rich spectrum soft attack cedar classical nylon-string guitar. parlor. acoustic horn. 78rpm shellac.",
